{"kind":"llm","key":"llm-2cf7825d5d1ffbdf59c79bdf6b9ae213d3c1fa279a9316b41e096e1c6c9e6d3d","recordedAt":"2026-11-10T06:11:39.811Z","liveMs":32118,"request":{"model":"gpt-5-mini-<date>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"You are the NIETE Teaching Assistant, a warm teacher coach. Below is a REAL coaching session. Produce a short \"commitment card\" the teacher receives on WhatsApp after our reflective conversation.\n\nWRITE ALL THREE TEXT FIELDS (commitment, action, lesson_label) IN ENGLISH — this teacher's lesson and our whole conversation were in English. Natural, warm, native English.\n\nGENDER-NEUTRAL — teachers are BOTH men and women. NEVER use gendered second-person verb forms. English is gender-neutral; address as \"you\".\n\nCODE-SWITCH LIKE A REAL TEACHER TEXTS. Pedagogical / technical / subject-matter terms MUST appear in ENGLISH (Latin letters) inline — NEVER translate them into English and NEVER transliterate them into English script. Teachers SAY these in English even mid-sentence: open-ended questions, conjunction, paragraph, pair reading, Think-Pair-Share, wait time, objective, model, fractions, percentage, group work, peer feedback.\nKeep terms natural; no transliteration needed.\n\nPLAIN LANGUAGE — the teacher must understand every word. Do NOT use coach-jargon she wouldn't say herself: \"scaffolding\", \"extension\", \"differentiation\", \"formative assessment\", \"higher-order thinking\", \"metacognition\", \"gradual release\". Describe the concrete move in plain words instead (e.g. instead of \"scaffolding\", write \"break it into small steps\"; instead of \"an extension\", write \"a harder task for the ones who finish early\").\n\nThe card has TWO parts:\n1. \"commitment\" — a single warm sentence (max ~18 words) in the teacher's OWN spirit. Reflect back what SHE values, drawn from her Q3 answer (her forward-looking reflection). Address her as \"you\"/\"we\". No honorifics, no name inside it.\n2. \"action\" — ONE specific, concrete thing to try in her NEXT class. It MUST be rooted in THIS exact lesson AND fuse her own value (from her Q3 answer + strengths) with THE TARGET below. Phrase it as an implementation intention anchored to next class (\"Next class, when [trigger], [do X]\") — but respect the gender-neutral rule above (imperative, not a gendered \"you will\"). Max ~32 words. Vivid and classroom-specific — name the actual materials/concept from THIS lesson. NOT generic.\n\nAlso return \"highlights\": an array of 2–4 short English keyword phrases that appear verbatim in \"action\" (concrete nouns) to visually emphasise. And \"lesson_label\": a 2–4 word English subject·topic label.\n\nSession (framework: FICO):\nTHE TARGET (fixed — do NOT choose a different area): indicator C3 \"Effective Feedback\". Why it is the next step: Feedback observed is mostly praise or restating answers; specific, actionable feedback will immediately help students revise mistakes and deepen learning. The scorer's suggested move: \"After a student answer, say one specific thing they did well and one instruction for improvement (e.g. 'Good count — write the numerator on top and try again').\". Your \"action\" is about THIS indicator only — ONE move, not a list of moves.\n\n- Her strengths: Concrete representations and real-life links\n- Growth areas: Effective Feedback (C3) — Feedback observed is largely praise and restatement; there are few instances of diagnostic guidance telling a student what to change next. Strategy: After a student answer, name one specific strength and give one clear next step (e.g., 'Good count — now write the numerator on top and try saying the fraction aloud'). || Wait-time & Inclusive Participation — Many students gave brief or choral replies; several capable students remained quiet or produced short answers. Strategy: Intentionally use a 3–5 second wait after asking a question; count silently or visually to yourself before accepting answers. || Differentiation (B6) — All students received the same task; no easier scaffolds or extension tasks were given. Strategy: Prepare two worksheet versions: one with simpler halves/quarters and one with extension comparing fractions. || Problem Solving / Productive Struggle (F5) — Tasks were routine identification and labelling without sustained thi…(+893)"}],"response_format":{"type":"json_object"}},"value":{"id":"gen-1794291067-TlOaeNt6kvCL1czcMZ4X","object":"chat.completion","created":1794291067,"model":"openai/gpt-5-mini","provider":"OpenAI","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":"default","choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"commitment\":\"We keep the steady pacing and give quieter students more wait time to explain using the fraction models.\",\"action\":\"Next class, when a student says 'above/below' on a fraction model, pause 3 seconds, say 'Good counting — you found the parts; now point to the numerator and say its number aloud.'\",\"lesson_label\":\"Math Fractions\",\"highlights\":[\"fraction model\",\"pause 3 seconds\",\"numerator\"]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Refining instructions**\n\nI’m noticing that the earlier version had 29 words, and now it's at 33. I could shorten it by swapping “wait 3 seconds” for “pause 3 seconds,” which has a similar count. It could be useful to remove “then” to tighten it up. I’m experimenting to get under 32 words by removing \"a\" before \"fraction model.\" The focus is on being vivid and academically relevant to the classroom. I think it’s looking great at 31 words!**Creating commitment sentence**\n\nI’m crafting a commitment sentence now. The earlier version said, \"We keep the steady pacing and give quieter students more wait time to explain using the fraction models.\" I want to highlight 2–4 key phrases that stay verbatim in the action, like “fraction model,” “pause 3 seconds,” “Good counting,” and “numerator.” I think I’ll choose three items: “fraction model,” “pau